---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<section class="sec">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num">&lt;100<small>MB</small></span>
        <span class="stat-label">Resident memory while playing</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num">0</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Analytics, crash reporters, or update pings</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num">100<small>%</small></span>
        <span class="stat-label">Rust. No Electron, no bundled browser</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <div class="container split">
    <div class="split-copy">
      <h2>Bit-perfect, when the output allows.</h2>
      <p>
        TIDE matches the device sample rate to the track, so nothing gets resampled
        when your hardware can play it natively. It does this without grabbing
        exclusive access, so other apps can still make sound.
      </p>
      <ul class="tiers">
        <li><span class="dot q-max"></span><strong class="q-max">MAX</strong> Up to 24-bit / 192 kHz FLAC</li>
        <li><span class="dot q-high"></span><strong class="q-high">HIGH</strong> 16-bit / 44.1 kHz lossless FLAC</li>
        <li><span class="dot q-low"></span><strong class="q-low">LOW</strong> AAC, for slow connections</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine">
        Tap the quality pill in the player bar to see the signal path from source to
        device, and why playback is or isn’t bit-perfect.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="signal-shot">
      <img
        src={`${base}screenshots/bit-perfect.png`}
        alt="TIDE signal path popup: a 24-bit 48 kHz FLAC source, sample rate conversion to 44.1 kHz, and MacBook Pro speakers, with the bit-perfect playback toggle"
        width="967"
        height="1226"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <div class="container split split-rev">
    <div class="tile net">
      <div class="term-bar">
        <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
        <span>outbound connections</span>
      </div>
      <div class="net-body">
        <div class="net-row"><span class="on">●</span><code>api.tidal.com</code><span class="muted">catalog &amp; playback</span></div>
        <div class="net-row"><span class="on">●</span><code>resources.tidal.com</code><span class="muted">artwork</span></div>
        <div class="net-row off"><span>○</span><code>analytics</code><span>none</span></div>
        <div class="net-row off"><span>○</span><code>crash reports</code><span>none</span></div>
        <div class="net-row off"><span>○</span><code>update checks</code><span>none</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="split-copy">
      <h2>I don’t want your data.</h2>
      <p>
        Session tokens and the library cache live on your Mac. Play counts stay local.
        TIDE only talks to TIDAL’s own endpoints for music, and nothing else.
      </p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>No account other than your TIDAL login</li>
        <li>No telemetry, ever</li>
        <li>Open source, so you can check</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <div class="container split">
    <div class="split-copy">
      <h2>Light enough to forget it’s running.</h2>
      <p>
        A GPU-drawn native UI that follows your display’s refresh rate, without
        shipping a browser to render a play button.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="tile ram">
      <div class="ram-row">
        <div class="ram-label"><span>TIDE</span><span>under 100 MB</span></div>
        <div class="ram-track"><span class="ram-bar tide" style="width: 8%"></span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ram-row">
        <div class="ram-label"><span class="muted">Official desktop client (Electron)</span><span class="muted">500 MB – 1.2 GB</span></div>
        <div class="ram-track"><span class="ram-bar electron" style="width: 100%"></span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="ram-note">Resident memory while playing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
